  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The axis is the second cervical vertebra, abbreviated as C2. Distinguished
    by its unique peg-like structure called the odontoid process or dens, it serves
    as a pivot point for the first cervical vertebra (atlas) to rotate around, enabling
    head rotation. The dens projects superiorly and fits into the anterior arch of
    the atlas, held in place by ligaments, allowing for the "no" head movement. Structurally
    robust, the axis is critical for both mobility and stability of the cervical
    region. Injury or misalignment of C2 can have serious consequences due to its
    proximity to the brainstem and spinal cord.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
