  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The sacrum is a large, triangular bone situated at the base of the vertebral
    column and upper, back part of the pelvic cavity. It's formed by the fusion of
    five sacral vertebrae during early adulthood. Superiorly, it articulates with
    the last lumbar vertebra, and inferiorly, it meets the coccyx. Laterally, the
    sacrum joins with the iliac bones of the pelvis at the sacroiliac joints. This
    bone serves as a structural intermediary, transmitting body weight from the spine
    to the pelvis and lower limbs. The sacrum also provides support and protection
    to pelvic organs. Due to its location and structure, the sacrum plays a pivotal
    role in providing stability and balance to the pelvis.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
