  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The inferior nasal conchae, also known as the inferior turbinates, are
    paired, curved bones that project from the lateral walls inside the nasal cavity.
    Distinct from the superior and middle nasal conchae (which are part of the ethmoid
    bone), the inferior nasal conchae stand as separate bones. Their primary role
    is to regulate airflow and increase the nasal cavity's surface area, aiding in
    humidifying, warming, and filtering inhaled air. The mucosa covering them contains
    blood vessels that help control nasal temperature and moisture. Enlargement or
    inflammation of the conchae, termed "turbinate hypertrophy," can result in nasal
    obstruction and reduced airflow.
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
