  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "\rThe right temporal bone is one of the two temporal bones located on
    the sides of the skull, contributing to the lateral walls and base of the cranial
    cavity. It is divided into several parts, including the squamous, mastoid, and
    petrous portions. The squamous part is flat and forms the temple, while the mastoid
    portion contains air cells and the petrous portion houses the inner ear structures.
    The temporal bone articulates with the sphenoid, parietal, and occipital bones.
    It also has various openings for nerves and blood vessels. Injuries to the temporal
    bone can affect hearing and balance due to its proximity to auditory and vestibular
    structures."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
